{"author":{"id":"417295a7cc0952a9a38b67be3a894daed91f9069f7407e038d12a4f9b9a8451e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792337695,"nonce":"XxqOk4pB1Oz5ZnWg3j5zakAyHQM=","title":"RayScript: the language, its VM and its fibers","message":"RayScript is the extension language of RayDE: hscript's logic (Haxe expressions, dynamic) and HXX-style markup in one language, with ashui's APIs and the editor's APIs for views and logic, able to bind native FFIs. The plan, the evidence for it and the decisions are in RayDE's git-bug 971d58e (read its comments; the latest supersedes the body). Decided by the user: our own parser and bytecode runtime in Rust, fibers the way Ash does them (krio, a poll at loop back-edges), Zyntax not a priority and Cranelift directly if a JIT is wanted; the collector follows Ash and WrenLift (WrenLift is now Immix only, non-moving, no write barrier). Taken as the plan's recommendations: the extension host is a separate process, native FFI needs a trust prompt for each library, type annotations are parsed for tools and not enforced, files are `.rays`.\n\nCrates: rayscript-syntax (lexer, parser, tree, diagnostics), rayscript-vm (compiler, GC, VM, fibers, standard library, host ABI), rayscript-cli (parse, check, run).\n\nBuilt:\n- rayscript-syntax: hscript's grammar as Haxe has it (precedence, lambdas, comprehensions, maps, switch with patterns, try, string interpolation in single quotes, metadata, optional types, imports), markup as expressions (elements, fragments, holes, `\u003cif\u003e`, `\u003cfor\u003e`, spread, JSX whitespace), `native \"lib\" { function ...; }` declarations; always a tree, with Error nodes, and diagnostics with spans and help; every prefix and mutation of a rich script parses without panic or a hang.\n\nNext, in order: the compiler (scope and captures, registers) and bytecode; the heap and collector; the VM with fibers (Task per fiber, epoch poll, fuel, cancel, host calls that park); the standard library (Std, Math, Array, String, Map, StringTools, Lambda, Reflect, Json); error spans and stack traces; the reactive core and the op stream for markup; the host process and its protocol; native FFI; hot reload; Cranelift if measured to be needed.","files":null}]}